leted":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nv4.6.stable.official [89cea1439]\n\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.6.stable - Linux Mint 22.1 (Xia) on X11 - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1660 (nvidia; 580.126.09) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i3-7100 CPU @ 3.90GHz (4 threads) - 31.30 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nAs the title says, `LookAtModifier3D` time based interpolation does not work when `Relative=true`, at least not in my test project. When exceeding the set angle limitation and reaching the \"flipping point\", the bone will immediately rotate to the opposite angle limit, instead of interpolating to it. With `Relative=false`, there is time based interpolation as expected.\n\nAt higher speeds of movement of the look at target, the time based interpolation does sometimes trigger with `Relative=true`, but only in one direction.\n\nHere's a video illustrating the issue, showing the difference in behaviour with Relative=true/false:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0238770c-33ba-44b6-a344-040c9d59517c\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nSee the MRP. It contains a humanoid skeleton with a `LookAtModifier3D` applied to the head bone. The `LookAtModifier3D` target is a `MeshInstance3D`, that can be rotated around the head of the skeleton using the left and right arrow keys. There is also a `CheckButton` to toggle the `Relative` option On/Off.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[look-at-mrp.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/24943417/look-at-mrp.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","